6. Overlapping Demand Theory and Vertical Specialization
Paper: International Economics (ECNCC301) | Unit: I | Topic: Overlapping demand theory, Vertical specialization-based trade
Introduction
Most world trade in manufactures is between similar rich countries, and much of it is intra-industry. Factor-endowment theory predicts the opposite. Two modern explanations are Linder's overlapping demand theory and trade based on vertical specialisation of production.
Linder's overlapping demand theory (1961)
Linder separated primary products, where trade follows endowments, from manufactures, where trade is determined by demand. A country's manufactured exports are the products for which there is a large domestic market, since firms first produce for the home market. The structure of domestic demand depends on per capita income, which shapes tastes for quality and sophistication. Hence two countries with similar per capita incomes have similar demand structures, with a large range of overlapping products that each can export to and import from the other. Trade intensity is therefore highest among countries with similar incomes, and trade between rich and poor countries is limited.
The theory explains intra-industry trade among industrial nations and the strong growth of trade among developed economies. Criticisms: it ignores supply-side factors and product differentiation as a source of trade, and its predictions are not always supported, as trade between unlike countries is substantial.
Vertical specialisation-based trade
Vertical specialisation (Hummels, Ishii and Yi, 2001) arises when production is split into stages across countries. A country imports intermediate inputs, adds value and exports the output, which may be further processed elsewhere. Conditions: at least two stages, goods crossing borders at least twice, and imported inputs embodied in exports. It results from falling transport and communication costs, trade liberalisation and multinational activity, and produces global value chains (electronics, automobiles, garments).
Effects: trade grows faster than output, countries specialise in tasks, not goods, and developing nations can join manufacturing chains without building entire industries. Risks include dependence on lead firms and vulnerability to supply disruptions.
Conclusion
Linder explains who trades with whom, and vertical specialisation explains what is traded and how production is organised. Both complement H-O in explaining modern trade.